# Git Basic Commands & Best Practices

Git is an open-source distributed version control system that tracks changes in source code during software development and enables multiple developers to work together without overwriting each other's contributions.

## Key Concepts: What Is Version Control?

Version control systems (VCS) track the history of file modifications, allowing teams or individuals:

- Track who changed what
  - When they made those edits  
    The timestamp for that change

Why it was introduced—helps when debugging problematic code deployments and allows easy rollbacks to previous stable versions.

Git is particularly popular because each developer has a complete repository copy (decentralized).

## Basic Workflow: Add, Stage & Commit Changes  

Working with Git involves three core steps:

```bash
git add .
```

Staging files puts them in the index area before committing—they become "what will be saved."

After staging your work:
  
  ```no-highlight bash
  
 git commit -m

# Clear description of what changed.
 
 # Example: Fix login bug, update README links
 
 ```
  

Commits are snapshots representing code state at that moment—use clear messages for easy navigation through history.

## Common Commands You'll Need Regularly:

```bash
git status              /* See unstaged changes */
```

- `diff` Unstaged diffs  
  - Compare staging area to HEAD

